{"about":"Lesson K21, threads, workgroups and memory. Computed by tools/school/fill_threads.mjs from the emulator in system/school/threads.mjs. Do not edit by hand. No GPU was used: every number is a count from the emulator's model or a published limit. Timings on a real GPU happen only in the lab, on the reader's device, labelled with their adapter.","sourceSha256":"542d91db03d9f15a7aab51d90bc51eaa222cabf67bdf9ba73efb39dd51953172","sourceFile":"system/school/threads.mjs","spec":{"about":"Published limits and rules, with their sources. WebGPU: W3C Candidate Recommendation Draft, https://www.w3.org/TR/webgpu/, section 3.6.2 Limits (the version read was dated 15 September 2026); the values are the defaults every WebGPU device must support, an adapter may offer more, and compat holds the compatibility-mode defaults. WGSL: https://www.w3.org/TR/WGSL/, section 13.3.1.1.4 global_invocation_id (workgroup_id * workgroup_size + local_invocation_id) and section 6.5.7 Out-of-Bounds Access (an invalid store may write any location of the bound GPUBuffer or not be executed; an invalid load may return any value of the buffer or zero). CUDA: CUDA Programming Guide 13.4.2, https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/: section 1 Programming Model (threads of a block are organized into groups of 32 threads called warps), section 2.3.4.1 Coalesced Global Memory Access (32-byte memory transactions; consecutive 4-byte words from a warp's 32 threads are 128 bytes fetched in four 32-byte transactions; the worst case is one transaction per thread), and the Compute Capabilities appendix (1,024 threads per block; block x and y up to 1,024, z up to 64; warp size 32). WebGPU has no warp: invocations are grouped into subgroups whose size the adapter reports (subgroupMinSize, subgroupMaxSize) when the subgroups feature is available.","webgpu":{"maxComputeWorkgroupSizeX":256,"maxComputeWorkgroupSizeY":256,"maxComputeWorkgroupSizeZ":64,"maxComputeInvocationsPerWorkgroup":256,"maxComputeWork